    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey can u explain how lactic acid actually works in your muscles during high intensity training? like why does it make them burn and how does the body get rid of it after the workout is over?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "During high-intensity training, your muscles produce lactic acid as a byproduct of anaerobic glycolysis—when they're working faster than oxygen can be delivered. It's not the acid itself that causes the burn, but the hydrogen ions it releases, which lower pH and trigger that stinging sensation. The body clears it post-workout via the Cori cycle, where the liver converts lactate back into glucose, and through oxidation in the heart and slow-twitch fibers. This process is usually complete within an hour or two, depending on intensity and recovery.\n\nA helpful mental model: think of lactic acid as a temporary \"battery\" that stores energy for later use, not just a waste product. The burn is your muscles' way of saying \"I'm working at my limit,\" and the cleanup is your body's way of recharging that battery.",
      "source": "target",
