    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "That crawling, lingering sensation is your body's cleanup crew working overtime—it's not just the ions, but the micro-tears and inflammation from the workout that keep the pain receptors firing. The hydrogen ions get buffered quickly, but the damaged muscle fibers release inflammatory chemicals (like prostaglandins) that sensitize those same nerves for hours. So the \"crawling\" is your immune system flooding the area with fluid and white blood cells, which physically presses on nerve endings. It's less a chemical alarm and more a biological construction site—the mess is real, and the cleanup takes time.\n\nThink of it like a sunburn: the initial sting is the UV damage, but the throbbing later is your skin's inflammatory response. Same idea here—the burn is the acute signal, the crawl is the aftermath. Your body is literally rebuilding the muscle, and that rebuilding process is noisy. It's not just a battery draining; it's a workshop with sparks flying.",
